UID:e91d1b4957b1f70f@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260822T014839Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 2027 MaineCare Eligibility Changes: Informa
 tion\, Resources\, and Partner Briefing
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			2027 MaineCare Eligibility Changes: Information\, Res
 ources\, and Partner Briefing\n2027 MaineCare Eligibility Changes: Infor
 mation\, Resources\, and Partner Briefing\nAug 21\, 2026\nThe federal gove
 rnment is making changes to MaineCare (Maine’s Medicaid program). These 
 changes apply to Medicaid programs across the country. Last week the O
 ffice of MaineCare Services (OMS) sent out a\nSave-the-Date\nfor an August
  24\, 2026\, partner and provider briefing on upcoming changes to Mai
 neCare eligibility. There is still time to\nregister\n!\nThe Office for F
 amily Independence (OFI) and OMS are working together to implement these
  changes as smoothly as possible to avoid disruptions in coverage for elig
 ible Mainers. Our goal is to provide Mainers with clear\, accurate\, and 
 actionable information so they can confidently understand how these change
 s may affect them.\nWhat is changing?\nAs of January 1\, 2027\, the foll
 owing MaineCare eligibility changes will start:\nFor MaineCare Expansion
  adults:\nNew Work Requirements\; and\nEligibility renewals every
  6 months instead of every 12 months.\nFor all MaineCare members:\nReduc
 tions in retroactive coverage.\nMaineCare Expansion members are adults be
 tween 19 and 64 years old who have MaineCare based on their income. Th
 ey do not meet the\nSocial Security disability guidelines\nor have Medicar
 e.\nWhat can MaineCare Members Do Now?\nThe most important things MaineCar
 e members can do right now are:\nKeep their contact information up to date
  with OFI\,\nRead any letters they receive\,\nContinue renewing their cove
 rage as required\, and\nContinue using their MaineCare benefits.\nWe know
  that members rely on their healthcare providers for trusted information a
 bout MaineCare. We appreciate the value that adds\, as well as the additi
 onal work. Our goal is to support providers through these changes\nResour
 ces to be aware of right now include:\nSave th\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dhhs
 /oms/providers/provider-bulletins/2027-mainecare-eligibility-changes-infor
 mation-resources-and-partner-briefing-2026-08-21
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/2027-maine
 care-eligibility-changes-information-resources-and-partner-briefing-2026-0
 8-21
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0a4fcd740666c090@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260821T185344Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260429
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] First Choice Women’s Resource Centers\, Inc
 . v. Davenport
DESCRIPTION:PRELIMINARY PRINT \nVolume 608 U. S. Part 1 \nPages 174–199 
 \nOFFICIAL REPORTS \nOF \nTHE SUPREME COURT \nApril 29\, 2026 \nREBECCA A.
  WOMELDORF \nreporter of decisions \nPage Proof Pending Publication\nNOTIC
 E: This preliminary print is subject to formal revision before \nthe bound
  volume is published. Users are requested to notify the Reporter \nof Deci
 sions\, Supreme Court of the United States\, Washington\, D. C. 20543\, \n
 pio@supremecourt.gov\, of any typographical or other formal errors. \nPage
  Proof Pending Publication\n174 OCTOBER TERM\, 2025 \nSyllabus \nFIRST CHO
 ICE WOMEN’S RESOURCE CENTERS\, \nINC. v. DAVENPORT\, ATTORNEY GENERAL OF
  \nNEW JERSEY \ncertiorari to the united states court of appeals for \nthe
  third circuit \nNo. 24–781. Argued December 2\, 2025—Decided April 29
 \, 2026 \nFirst Choice Women's Resource Centers\, Inc.\, is a religious no
 nproft orga-\nnization that has provided counseling and resources to pregn
 ant women \nin New Jersey since 1985. Believing that life begins at concep
 tion\, the \ngroup does not provide abortions or refer clients to others f
 or abortions. \nIn 2022\, New Jersey's Attorney General established a “R
 eproductive \nRights Strike Force” that issued a consumer alert accusing
  groups like \nFirst Choice of seeking to prevent people from accessing re
 productive \nhealth care by providing false or misleading abortion informa
 tion. The \nAttorney General served a subpoena on First Choice\, commandin
 g the \ngroup to produce 28 categories of documents\, including documents 
 re-\nfecting the names\, phone numbers\, addresses\, and places of employm
 ent \nof all individuals who had made donations to First Choice by any mea
 ns \nother than through one specifc webpage. Effectively\, that demand re-
 \nquired First Choice to provide personal information about donors who \ng
 ave through two other websites\, through the group's various social \nmedi
 a pages\, by mail\, in person\, or by any other means. The subpoena \nwarn
 ed twice that failure to comply may render the group liable for \ncontempt
  of court and oth\nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us1r30_3
 f14.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/608us1r30_3f14.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
