 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ The Employme
 nt Situation in Maine - July 2026\nServices\nJobs & Training\nUnemployment
  Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor Laws\nLabor Stati
 stics\nWorkforce Development\nThe Employment Situation in Maine - July 202
 6\nAugust 21\, 2026\nReleased: 10 a.m. Friday\, August 21\, 2026\nContact:
  Mark McInerney\, (207) 620-0197\nThe Employment Situation in Maine - July
  2026\nThe 3.1 unemployment rate was unchanged for the fourth consecutive 
 month. Nonfarm wage and salary jobs have remained within a narrow range fo
 r 27 consecutive months.\nSeasonally Adjusted Statewide Labor Force Estima
 tes\nThe unemployment rate remained at 3.1 percent in July. Unemployment r
 ates have remained within a narrow range between 3.1 and 3.3 percent for 2
 3 consecutive months. Unemployment has been below four percent for 55 mont
 hs\, the longest duration on record. Maine's unemployment rate has been be
 low the U.S. average for more than 18 years\, and remains below the long-t
 erm average of 5.4 percent for the state since 1976. Unemployment averaged
  4.2 percent for New England and 4.1 percent for the U.S. in the month.\nT
 he labor force participation rate averaged 59 percent in the last three mo
 nths\, modestly lower than the three months through April. The employment-
 to-population ratio was also modestly lower in the three months through Ju
 ly compared to the three months prior.\nSeasonally Adjusted Statewide Nonf
 arm Jobs Estimates\nPreliminary seasonally adjusted estimates indicate tha
 t in July\, nonfarm wage and salary jobs were little changed from June. Mo
 st sectors exhibited little net job change over-the-month.\nJob estimates 
 have stayed within a narrow range near 660\,000 for 27 consecutive months 
 (es\nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13358888
